WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.339
Picture the payments app on an ordinary day.

2
00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:13.017
Payments arrive in a line, and a program called the ledger takes them one at a time, in order, and keeps each account's balance.

3
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:19.468
The line is kept by Kafka, a program that stores records in order, for other programs to read.

4
00:00:19.468 --> 00:00:22.417
The ledger keeps a bookmark: how far it got.

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.262
Now the card's bank, which has to say yes to each payment, stops answering.

6
00:00:27.262 --> 00:00:28.381
For three minutes.

7
00:00:28.381 --> 00:00:30.618
Thirty payments arrive every second.

8
00:00:30.618 --> 00:00:33.975
What should the ledger do with the one in front of it?

9
00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:35.156
Keep that question.

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.518
Let's step into a kitchen for a minute.

11
00:00:37.518 --> 00:00:40.988
Order slips hang on a rail, and the cook takes them in order.

12
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:47.608
A smudged slip that nobody can read goes onto a problem tray, with a note, and the cook moves on.

13
00:00:47.608 --> 00:00:51.062
That's a permanent failure: trying again won't help.

14
00:00:51.062 --> 00:00:54.648
In the app, it's pay-3, whose amount came in as 12x50.

15
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.336
But if the oven isn't hot yet, the slip is fine.

16
00:00:58.336 --> 00:01:00.399
Wait a moment and try again.

17
00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:02.388
That's a transient failure.

18
00:01:02.388 --> 00:01:08.651
The ledger does the same with a slow bank: wait 100 milliseconds, then 200, then 400.

19
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:20.753
So the rail is one partition of the payments log, the cook is the ledger, and the tray is a separate log for records it gave up on, a dead-letter topic, called payments.DLT.

20
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:24.321
Now back to the bank, down for three minutes.

21
00:01:24.321 --> 00:01:29.215
Waits of 100, 200 and 400 milliseconds give up in under a second.

22
00:01:29.215 --> 00:01:31.323
So pay-5 goes onto the tray.

23
00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:33.205
And so does the next one.

24
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:35.574
Thirty a second, for three minutes.

25
00:01:35.574 --> 00:01:38.223
How many good payments end up on the tray?

26
00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:39.232
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.144
Five thousand four hundred.

28
00:01:44.144 --> 00:01:51.227
Thirty times a hundred and eighty seconds, every one a good payment, waiting for a person to send it back by hand.

29
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:53.693
That's not a problem tray any more.

30
00:01:53.693 --> 00:01:55.114
That's a problem room.

31
00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:58.536
So quick retries are for a hiccup, not for an outage.

32
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.236
Fine, then wait longer.

33
00:02:00.236 --> 00:02:03.308
Thirty seconds between tries, up to six tries.

34
00:02:03.308 --> 00:02:05.845
That gives the bank time to come back.

35
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:11.839
But now the cook stands at the oven with pay-5 for three minutes, and every slip behind it waits.

36
00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:15.909
And Kafka gives a reader five minutes between two requests for more.

37
00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:18.363
After that, it treats the reader as gone.

38
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:20.310
Short waits flood the tray.

39
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.152
Long waits hold up the rail.

40
00:02:22.152 --> 00:02:22.613
Well...

41
00:02:22.613 --> 00:02:24.784
neither is the kitchen we wanted.

42
00:02:24.784 --> 00:02:29.916
What we want is for pay-5 to wait somewhere else, while the rail keeps moving.

43
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.557
Can't the ledger skip pay-5 and come back to it later?

44
00:02:33.557 --> 00:02:36.887
A queue program can hold one message back like that.

45
00:02:36.887 --> 00:02:39.448
But Kafka's bookmark is a single number.

46
00:02:39.448 --> 00:02:43.353
Move it past pay-5, and you've told Kafka that pay-5 is done.

47
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.305
A good kitchen has a second rail, for slips that wait on something.

48
00:02:47.305 --> 00:02:51.961
The cook pins the slip there with a time on it, and carries on with the main rail.

49
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:58.182
In Kafka, the second rail is another topic, a separate log, called payments.retry-10s.

50
00:02:58.182 --> 00:03:06.599
The ledger writes pay-5 there, waits for Kafka to confirm it, moves its bookmark past it, and carries on at full speed.

51
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:09.595
Every record carries the time it was written.

52
00:03:09.595 --> 00:03:15.377
So a reader knows pay-5 is due ten seconds after that, and the one behind it a little later.

53
00:03:15.533 --> 00:03:19.888
A second reader, in a group of its own, reads the retry topic.

54
00:03:19.888 --> 00:03:22.065
Its first record isn't due yet.

55
00:03:22.065 --> 00:03:23.048
Does it sleep?

56
00:03:23.048 --> 00:03:23.259
No.

57
00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:26.489
A long sleep breaks the same five-minute rule.

58
00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:30.840
Instead, it pauses that partition, and keeps asking Kafka for more.

59
00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.863
A paused partition hands back nothing, but asking keeps the reader in its group.

60
00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:41.545
At ten seconds, it resumes, goes back to pay-5, and calls the bank again.

61
00:03:41.545 --> 00:03:42.375
Still down?

62
00:03:42.375 --> 00:03:48.491
Then pay-5 moves one step down, to a topic with a longer wait, payments.retry-1m.

63
00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:50.289
The last step is the tray.

64
00:03:50.289 --> 00:03:54.282
Steps with growing waits, one under the other, like a staircase.

65
00:03:54.282 --> 00:03:56.153
These are called retry topics.

66
00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:59.780
When the bank comes back, the main rail carries on at once.

67
00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.813
The records on the steps go through on their next try.

68
00:04:02.813 --> 00:04:05.959
Only what still fails at the last step reaches the tray.

69
00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:10.123
One rule keeps the retry reader simple: each topic has one wait.

70
00:04:10.123 --> 00:04:17.105
Every record on the ten-second rail waits the same ten seconds, so they come due in the order they were written.

71
00:04:17.105 --> 00:04:19.785
The reader only ever watches the first one.

72
00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:20.839
And one cost.

73
00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:22.584
Notice what just happened.

74
00:04:22.584 --> 00:04:27.014
pay-7 went through first, while pay-5 was still waiting on a step.

75
00:04:27.014 --> 00:04:29.430
Say they belong to the same account.

76
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.712
Its payments are now out of order.

77
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:34.387
For a receipt email, that does no harm.

78
00:04:34.387 --> 00:04:36.842
For a payment and its refund, it does.

79
00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:40.655
Keeping an account in order is the next part of the lesson.

80
00:04:40.833 --> 00:04:43.869
Here's the heart of it, in Java and in Kotlin.

81
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:50.656
The ledger sends the payment to the retry topic, waits for the answer, and only then commits its bookmark.

82
00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:54.423
The retry reader adds ten seconds to the record's time.

83
00:04:54.423 --> 00:04:56.838
Not due yet: pause, and keep polling.

84
00:04:56.838 --> 00:04:59.515
Due: resume, and seek back to the record.

85
00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:04.822
So, what should the ledger do with the payment in front of it while the bank is down?

86
00:05:04.822 --> 00:05:07.731
Not stand at the oven, and not throw it on the tray.

87
00:05:07.731 --> 00:05:11.479
Pin it to a second rail with a time, and keep the main rail moving.

88
00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:13.479
Quick retries for a hiccup.

89
00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.324
Retry topics for an outage.

90
00:05:15.324 --> 00:05:17.648
The tray for what's really broken.

91
00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:19.200
One quick check.

92
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:26.330
Someone puts a ten-minute record and a ten-second record on the same retry topic, the ten-minute one first.

93
00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:29.062
When does the ten-second one get its try?

94
00:05:29.062 --> 00:05:30.128
Pause and think.

95
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:34.443
After ten minutes.

96
00:05:34.443 --> 00:05:39.932
The reader only watches the first record, so the short wait is stuck behind the long one.

97
00:05:39.932 --> 00:05:42.399
That's why each wait gets its own topic.
